ocus on after the war  because there's an intriguing question  here um which which is well  doesn't it rather depend on who wins and  what a win  looks like because  to put it simply  is this plan likely to happen is this  are the funds likely to flow  if russia is in charge of ukraine  absolutely this is the question all of  this is predicated on victory and uh a  number of speakers today at the  conference made it crystal clear that we  need to support ukraine now both  financially in terms of weapons uh so  that their construction a is cheaper b  makes sense at all of course uh if  weapons are not forced coming uh we  might be in a situation there's not much  to recover right so you're absolutely  correct  right so  the the the funds and the plan that  depends up presumably then just looking  at what victory might look like would  that depend on russia giving up  territory that it has taken  well you know russia is not going to  give up anything so it has to be taken  away from russia  or there should be you know they're  essentially a couple of uh  outcomes one can think of one is uh  ukraine will be able with help of  international partners and friends to  recover territories or ukraine won't be  able uh immediately to recover this  territories uh but some will be and the  front lines will stabilize somewhere and  this was a discussion too what happens  if the front lines stabilize for a while  and the  war becomes protracted prolonged how  should we go about this and so there are  multiple scenarios  you you mentioned um corruption was was  one of the things that that  will come up at this conference because  of course before russia invaded there  were widespread concerns about a  corruption in ukraine reconstruction is  going to mean  billions and billions of euros are  flowing into and out of the country do  you accept that there that there will be  continuing uh concern amongst uh  potential  accreditors and investors about where  and how this money is accounted for  there are two concerns one is corruption  within ukraine and the second concern is  lobbyism and getting the preferred  contractors which are politically  connected to different donors uh and the  the first concern undermines the  transparency and trust into the effort  and the second concern just makes their  construction effort very very expensive  so both have to be addressed and for  that uh there are been multiple  proposals what kind of governance  mechanisms can address them uh the  important aspect is to understand that  these issues are concentrated in the  area of procurement first of all so  making procurement uh very clear very  specific very transparent will help  and will go a long way thank you so much  for outlining that so clearly uh timothy  uh  milivanov economic advisers to ukrainian  president uh vladimir zelenki thank you

